<<~ ahu slot=`#/red-1-and-red-2-one-root-cause-the-product-was-w`>>

!! Red 1 (node-b surface-mount-real-island, `acked: false`) and Red 2 (two-phase-boot T5, faceless herm floor): one root cause, the product was wrong
CI log line: `teardown-fault: the island could not tear down: unsaved at teardown: "$:/StoryList"`. The same fault shows in port-page-real-island, verb-tiddler-dispatch and the browser daemon worker.

''Cause.'' Since 407f3251d the kernel opens the default story after the outbound bridge stands. Before that, TW5's `story` startup wrote `$:/StoryList` before the shore was resident, so no save followed. Now the opener's write reaches the bridge as a hand. The cascade routes it through `current-wiki-personal`. At a composition with no personal layer, the save is refused:
* at the daemon floor the pointer names `wikis/daemon/personal`, a bag that floor does not mount;
* at the surface, nothing mounts it.

The refused save raises an unrouted-save alert for a write no person made and stays owed in the drain. Teardown then names it unsaved, and the vessel reads the teardown as faulted. Floor replay (glass2) played no part.

''Cure'' (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/island-story.ts`, new; `island-adaptor.ts`; `sovereign-kernel.ts`):
* `IslandAdaptor.routesSave(title)` reads the same verdict as `_settle` without side effects: no alert and no console line.
* `IslandAdaptor.keepInEngine(title)` records the current wiki value as landed with no bag, so the bridge reads the opener's change as foam.
* `openIslandStory(tw5, adaptor)`:
** a composition with a layer for the story saves it through the bridge, as before;
** one with none keeps `$:/StoryList` and the `$:/HistoryList` entry in the engine only.

A faceless floor now tears down clean. The herm path is untouched. Explicit hand saves that refuse still refuse by name, as before.

''Tests.'' New: `packages/lararium-tw5/tests/island-story-owes-no-save.test.ts`. It runs over the real wiki and the real bridge:
* no layer for the story: the probe raises nothing, no store hears the story, no alert is raised, and `stop()` returns `[]`;
* CONTROL: with a personal layer, the story saves to it;
* CONTROL: the bare opener leaves `$:/StoryList` owed. This reproduces the fault the cure removes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/red-3-the-product-was-wrong-the-weld-reads-the-r`>>

!! Red 3 (held-out weld M5 mirror-edit): the product was wrong, the weld reads the right place
''Diagnosis.'' A local smoke drove the steps W3 and M5 take: the worker sidebar set to Recent, then a new tiddler typed in the mirror. A page-side port trace showed:
* the mirror's `port:write` lands: `port:written status=applied`, and `port:changes` echoes the title. So the write does land at the pure-local browser vessel with a bound face;
* the worker re-projects: 12 → 15 frames;
* but the worker's Recent timeline painted `<div class="tc-menu-list-item"></div>`: one empty day.

The trace showed why. The mirror sent `modified = "Wed Nov 11 2026 18:56:37 GMT-0800 (Pacific Standard Time)"`. `glass-port-adaptor.ts` `ownFields` used `String(value)` on a Date, and joined lists without brackets. No engine parses that date back, so the worker held the tiddler with an invalid date. `eachday` made one group, but the date view and the `sameday` list rendered nothing. The same bad bytes also reach the store.

The "unrouted `$:/StoryList` personal save at pure local boot" console line was red 1's fault, unrelated to M5. It is gone now too.

''Cure.'' `ownFields` reads TW5's own `getFieldStrings()`. For a bare fields object it falls back to TW5 form: `YYYYMMDDHHMMSSmmm` UTC dates and `[[bracketed]]` list members.

''Tests.'' New vector in `glass-port-adaptor.test.ts`, `glass-writes-tw5-field-strings`, over the real island rig. Red first, proven: it received the locale string against the old code. Green after the cure: the store holds `20261112025637123` and `plain [[two words]]`, and the island engine reads the date back.

''Smoke after the cure.'' `WORKER FLOOR SHOWS TITLE: true`, the worker Recent reads "11th November 2026 smoke-mirror-…", and the wire carries `modified=20261112025950541`.

<<~/ahu>>

